  <section>
    <h2>West Paris Dispensary Overview</h2>
    <p>West Paris is a small town along Route 26 in the western Maine foothills, between South Paris and Bethel. The town's adult-use retail market is a single storefront (West Paris Mercantile) on Bethel Rd that serves a wide Oxford County catchment that includes Paris, Norway, and parts of western Maine heading toward Sunday River. West Paris is well-positioned for outdoor recreation visitors passing through on Route 26 to the Sunday River ski area or heading north toward Rangeley.</p>
    <p>West Paris Mercantile operates a traditional general-store format with a cannabis counter in the same building, giving the town a one-stop-shop option. The menu runs the standard adult-use and medical selection — flower, pre-rolls, edibles, vapes, and concentrates — at rural pricing.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Dispensaries in West Paris</h2>
    <p>The table below lists the licensed adult-use storefront currently operating in West Paris. Hours, menus, and pricing change frequently — always verify directly with the operator before making the drive.</p>
    <table aria-label="Licensed dispensaries in West Paris, Maine">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Operator</th><th scope="col">Address</th><th scope="col">Website</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>West Paris Mercantile, LLC</td><td>253 Bethel Rd Unit A</td><td>Westparismercantile.com</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>All storefronts are licensed by the Maine Office of Cannabis Policy (OCP) for both adult-use and medical sales. Medical patients pay only the 5.5% state sales tax; adult-use customers pay the 10% state excise plus 5.5% sales tax on top of sticker price.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What to Expect on a First Visit</h2>
    <p>If you are 21 or older with a valid government-issued photo ID (Maine driver's license, out-of-state ID, or passport), you can walk into any adult-use dispensary in West Paris and purchase. The process is the same as buying alcohol — ID check at the door, browse the menu, transact at the counter, and walk out with a sealed exit bag.</p>
    <p>First-time visitors should expect a brief intake — the <a href="/guides/maine-cannabis-staffing-licensing">budtender</a> will ask if you have used cannabis before, what effects you are looking for, and whether you have any preferences on format (flower, edible, vape, concentrate). West Paris's general-store format means you can also pick up coffee, snacks, and supplies at the same time.</p>
    <p>For medical patients, bring your Maine medical cannabis card (or a valid out-of-state card if your state has a reciprocity agreement with Maine). Medical patients have access to higher-dose products and pay only the 5.5% state sales tax — a meaningful discount on larger purchases.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Pricing in West Paris</h2>
    <p>Pricing at West Paris dispensaries tracks the rural Oxford County market, with the usual seasonal and competitive variation. As of mid-2026:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Flower (eighth, 3.5g):</strong> $30-50, premium indoor can run higher</li>
      <li><strong>Pre-rolls (single 1g):</strong> $12-18, multi-packs $25-50</li>
      <li><strong>Edibles (100mg THC pack):</strong> $20-35, depending on format and brand</li>
      <li><strong>Distillate cartridges (1g):</strong> $35-50</li>
      <li><strong>Concentrates (1g):</strong> $40-80, depending on quality and format</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Medical patients save the 10% state excise — a meaningful discount on larger purchases. Adult-use customers should ask about first-time buyer promotions, birthday discounts, and weekday specials, which are common in this market.</p>
  </section>

